/target
/docs/notes/
/docs/plans/
/docs/superpowers/

# proptest saves the shrunk input of a failing property beside the test source. Ignoring all of
# them made a counterexample found on one machine unreplayable on every other one, which is how
# rustfs/gateway#226 stayed a random ambush on unrelated branches for days; proptest's own header
# in the file recommends checking it in. So a seed that sits beside a checked-in integration test
# is a fixture and is kept, and everything else — spikes, scratch crates, anything outside a
# tests/ directory — stays a local debugging aid.
#
# Keeping a seed is the weaker half of the discipline, not the whole of it: a counterexample you
# have diagnosed belongs in a named test that says what is wrong and names the issue, because
# that replays deterministically and cannot go stale against a generator that moved. The seed is
# what carries the ones nobody has diagnosed yet.
#
# One consequence to know before it surprises you: mutation testing a property writes a seed for
# the mutant, which is junk the moment the mutation is reverted. Delete it; do not commit it.
*.proptest-regressions
!crates/*/tests/*.proptest-regressions
